 type path when a typedef changed). A run that re-parses most of a big project drops the old rows
 in one pass per table and resolves everything again.
 
+**C++ member calls.** A C++ call through a receiver (`e.start()`, `p->start()`, `b.engine.start()`) stores the receiver the same way as a type path with the class as `Q:Engine` (`ts_ref.rtype`, `Q:Boat|engine|start`): the declared type of a local, parameter (value, pointer, reference, `const`), file-level variable or field (smart pointers `unique_ptr`/`shared_ptr` name their argument), the type an `auto` initializer builds (`Engine()`, `new Engine`, `Engine{}`, `std::make_unique<Pump>()`, a cast), a function's return type, and, inside a method, a bare name no scope declares (a member of its class) and `this`. Fields give the next class, through typedefs and `using` aliases. When the path ends in a class that defines the method itself (a class that only inherits it falls back to name scoring), the call goes to that class's method and no other, certain; overloads of one class are one target, same-name classes in two namespaces a guess. Any other receiver stays a guess by name. Members of a struct in a `.h` file (parsed as C) type paths too. Schema 25.
+
 **Change detection on big trees** (`walkcache`). A tree of at least 50,000 files keeps its last
 walk in `$FOXDB_HOME/walk/<project id>`: the directories it entered with their modification
 times (nanoseconds), the files in each with size and mtime, the notes on directories it left out,
